There are 3 suggested ways in these papers to minimize coherency chatter and minimize coherency
stall.

<a> Speculative Coherence
<b> Coherence Decoupling
<c> Coherence Event Prediction
...........

<a> Speculative Coherence
    Use Coherence Directory or a separate Directory to hash PC+addr or addr or PC to predict
    coherent value.

    My addition to this proposal was to use inline caches to store data prefetch information.
    This solves TLB misses, cache misses and coherence cost all in one

<b> Coherence Decoupling
    Differentiate between time to get cached value vs time to get permission to use the value.
    
    Speculate on returned value and continue forward. If after gaining permission the new 
    value doesn't match old value then your load was stale, squash dependent instructions.

    Ohh btw ... this solves a few other coherency miss latency problems too:
    > False Sharing : Because the before-after values will match

    > Silent Stores : Write to the same line from another core that doesn't update the value
    
    > Temporarily Silent Stores: Another core writes data but reverts it back. Now this
      I m not so sure of ... needs more thinking!!

<c>  Coherence Event Prediction
     Classic OoO style of execution. Use a precommit store buffer and load buffer.
     PSB and Load don't have to pay for coherence. Load can use Coherence Decoupling
     to avoid coherence stall whereas PSB is not coherent. At some point commit 
     instructions atomically and if other cores have written to memory that you 
     reordered past, rollback.


